  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nTonight/Labor Day.\n\nOvernight, the ridge axis shifts further east towards the Great\nLakes as a shortwave advances out of the Northern Plains. \nMoisture profiles begin to saturate leading to a slight increase\nin PoPs for the overnight and early morning hours across \nnorthern MN. A few thunderstorms will be possible as well, but \nthe environment is lacking deep layer shear which will keep \nstorms on the tame side. Through the remainder of the holiday \nthe Northland will find itself in the warm sector of an \napproaching system in the Northern Rockies. The continued warm \nair advection and isentropic forcing may lead to off and on rain\nchances through the day with mostly cloudy skies. Temperatures \nwill be in the 70s with a few low 80s possible. Additionally, \nthe southerly winds will see our dew points climb into the upper\n60s by the afternoon.\n\nLate Labor Day into Tuesday:\n\nLarge scale ascent is expected to increase late Labor Day and \nthrough Tuesday morning. An upper level trough over the Canadian \nPrairies pushes the ridge completely out of the Northland opening the \ndoor for southwesterly flow to stream back into the region. One of \nthe unique aspects of this incoming set up is the high PWATs that \nthe system will have access to. The IVT plots would suggest \ncontributions from both the the Gulf and Hurricane Marie. \nClimatology puts the incoming PWATs near the max of climatology.\nDespite this wealth of moisture, most of the guidance keeps QPF\nfairly moderated. EFI is mainly honing in on areas north of the\nInternational Border as being the hotpots for heavy rainfall, \nlikely coinciding with the warm frontal boundary. While we may \nnot be the recipients of the highest rain totals we should still\nsee our fair share with current guidance suggesting around an \n1\" for most of MN with lower amounts across the Arrowhead and NW\nWI. \n\nSevere chances start to increase late Monday as well with an upper \nlevel jet nudging in from the southwest. Instability may be limited \nwith high PWATs and lack of mid level lapse rates. With these meager \ningredients in play the SPC has out western counties outlined in a \nmarginal risk (threat level 1 out of 5). Primary hazard would likely \nbe hail the size of quarters with damaging winds being a secondary \nthreat. Storm activity could be persistent into Tuesday as a low \nlevel jet strengthens across the Midwest. \n\nTuesday will see ongoing showers and storms through the morning. The \nupper level low over the Canadian Prairies will strengthen with a \nnoticeable dry slot sweeping in behind the cold front set to move \nthrough the region. Depending on how quickly the front advances\neast the region we could have some lingering severe storm\nthreat into NW WI in the afternoon. \n\nWed - Fri:\n\nOn Wednesday the upper level low spins into the Hudson Bay with \npersistent cyclonic flow aloft over the region. Low level moisture \nstill looks favorable with mid and upper level moisture questionable \nat the moment. The pattern looks pretty similar to the ones we get \nwith isolated showers and more widespread drizzle. Overall, \nexpecting a cloudy day with much cooler temperatures. Highs may not \nclimb out of the 70s in northern MN with NW WI in the mid and low \n70s. Thursday looks to be the quietest weather day in the upcoming \nweek with surface high pressure gliding across the Midwest. Rain \nchances look to return Friday as another front cross through the \nNorthland.",
